  </header>

  <section class="article-header">
    <div class="article-meta">
      <span class="article-meta__section">Flusher · Everyday</span>
      <span class="article-meta__rule"></span>
      <span>Investigation Nº 06</span>
      <span class="article-meta__rule"></span>
      <span>Published November 8, 2026</span>
      <span class="article-meta__rule"></span>
      <span>6 min read</span>
    </div>

    <p class="kicker">For the woman whose face has started to answer things she did not know were speaking to it.</p>

    <h1 class="hero-headline">What a hot drink actually <em>does</em> to your face.</h1>

    <p class="standfirst">An investigation into eight ordinary things you meet before noon — a mug, a meeting room, a video call, a cleanser tube, a "cooling" serum — and what each of them, measurably, does on the way past.</p>

    <div class="byline">
      <span class="byline__name">By E. Cotswold</span>
      <span>The Daily Body</span>
      <span>Explained, not advised</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <figure class="hero-photo">
    {%- if s.hero != blank -%}
      {{ s.hero | image_url: width: 1600 | image_tag:
           widths: '640, 960, 1280, 1600',
           sizes: '(min-width: 1184px) 1120px, 92vw',
           loading: 'eager',
           alt: 'A woman in a grey long-sleeve top in an office corridor, holding a steaming ceramic mug close under her chin with both hands, looking down.' }}
    {%- endif -%}
    <figcaption>
      <span class="fig-tag">Cover</span>
      <span>The mug is the most common trigger a flusher meets before nine in the morning. It is also the trigger nobody warns her about, because a mug does not look like a chilli pepper. To her nervous system, it is one.</span>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <main class="article-body">

    <div class="prose">
      <p>Most of what happens to a flusher's face on a given morning is caused by objects and rooms she does not think of as triggers. A mug of tea. A room warmed by too many bodies. The small tile in the corner of a video call showing her own face. A cleanser marketed as gentle. A serum marketed as cooling. Each of these has a specific, measurable effect on the tissue of her face, and most of the effects are counter-intuitive enough that a reasonable adult can go decades without connecting them.</p>

      <p>This piece is the sixth in a Daily Body series investigating what ordinary objects and rooms actually do to the body. As with the previous five, we are not advising anyone to do anything. We are just explaining what the object is doing.</p>
    </div>

    <aside class="identify" role="note">
      <p class="identify__label">If any of this is true this morning, keep reading:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your face was already pink before you finished the first cup of coffee.</li>
        <li>You noticed, on a video call yesterday, that the flush arrived roughly a minute after the self-view tile did.</li>
        <li>You have a serum on your shelf that tingles, and you have been trusting the tingle.</li>
        <li>You have wondered, more than once, whether the problem is that you are simply too sensitive a person.</li>
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <section class="entry">
      <p class="entry__object">Object one</p>
      <h2 class="entry__title">The mug reaches your face before the tea does.</h2>
      <div class="entry__body">
        <p>A ceramic mug at eighty degrees Celsius held six inches under the chin transfers roughly the same radiant heat to the perioral skin that a small space heater transfers at half a metre. That heat activates TRPV1 — the same receptor a chilli pepper binds<a href="#tdb-fn1" class="fn">1</a>. In skin with a normal threshold, the receptor's response is silent. In skin whose threshold has drifted lower, the same input produces the same signal a jalapeño produces on the tongue. The vessel opens. The colour arrives. In studies using thermal imaging, the interval between mug and colour is well under ninety seconds.</p>
        <p>The mug is not doing anything special. The threshold is what has changed. That is the pattern for almost every trigger in this piece: the input is ordinary. The response has moved.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry">
      <p class="entry__object">Object two</p>
      <h2 class="entry__title">A room can trigger a flush before anyone speaks.</h2>
      <div class="entry__body">
        <p>A conference room warmed by nine bodies and one radiator sits about three degrees above what the vasomotor system reads as neutral. In a flusher, that three-degree difference is a trigger. The face has begun to redden by the time the meeting agenda is opened. Nothing has been said. Nothing has been eaten. The room itself is the input.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="stat-callout">
        <div class="stat-callout__num">19%</div>
        <div class="stat-callout__text">of women in a <strong>2011 National Rosacea Society workplace survey</strong> (n=1,134) reported believing that a visible facial flush had cost them, specifically, a promotion. Not the meeting content. The temperature of the room.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry">
      <p class="entry__object">Object three</p>
      <h2 class="entry__title">The mirror inside the screen is its own trigger.</h2>
      <div class="entry__body">
        <p>Video calls do something unusual: they give a flusher a live mirror while she is speaking. The self-view — the small tile of her own face in the corner — measurably increases sympathetic activation compared to the same call with self-view hidden. The pathway is visual feedback into limbic response into adrenergic release. The face reddens. The screen shows it. The screen makes it worse.</p>
        <p>The single most effective adjustment reported by flushers on r/Rosacea is not a product. It is a two-tap operation on the meeting software: right-click, hide self-view. The face still reddens on the input. It reddens less on the loop.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="poll" data-poll="which-flush" role="group" aria-label="Which flush pattern is yours?">
      <p class="poll__eyebrow">Reader poll · Instant</p>
      <p class="poll__question">Which one is your flush?</p>
