    <section class="entry">
      <p class="entry__object">Object four</p>
      <h2 class="entry__title">Eighty-two per cent of patients react to products marketed as gentle.</h2>

      <figure class="entry__photo">
        {%- if s.cleanser != blank -%}
          {{ s.cleanser | image_url: width: 1200 | image_tag:
               widths: '480, 760, 960, 1200',
               sizes: '(min-width: 960px) 900px, 92vw',
               loading: 'lazy',
               alt: 'A pair of hands squeezing a plain white tube over a bathroom sink, one hand holding the small cap.' }}
        {%- endif -%}
        <figcaption>
          <span class="fig-tag">Fig. 2</span>
          <span>A tube marketed for sensitive skin. Whether the tube is gentle for the person holding it is a separate question from whether the tube is gentle in the average.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="entry__body">
        <p>In a 2023 National Rosacea Society survey of 1,023 members<a href="#tdb-fn2" class="fn">2</a>, 82% of respondents reported reacting to at least one product labelled for sensitive skin. The cleanser the reader had tolerated for ten years did not, in most cases, change its formulation. Her barrier did.</p>
        <p>The word "gentle" on a label is not measured against her specific barrier state. It is measured against a market average. When a barrier begins to drift out of that average — quietly, over a period of months, as most rosacea-adjacent barriers do — the same product she tolerated last year will burn this year. The product did not become harsher. She became more accurate at reporting what harsh feels like.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pullquote">
        <p>You are not too sensitive. Your barrier is.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry">
      <p class="entry__object">Object five</p>
      <h2 class="entry__title">The tingle is not cooling. It is TRPV1 firing.</h2>

      <figure class="entry__photo">
        {%- if s.serum != blank -%}
          {{ s.serum | image_url: width: 1200 | image_tag:
               widths: '480, 760, 960, 1200',
               sizes: '(min-width: 960px) 900px, 92vw',
               loading: 'lazy',
               alt: 'Extreme close-up of skin with a droplet of clear serum on the surface, edged with a faint cool highlight.' }}
        {%- endif -%}
        <figcaption>
          <span class="fig-tag">Fig. 3</span>
          <span>A cooling serum on rosacea-prone skin. The sensation in the first ninety seconds is a receptor event, not a temperature event.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="entry__body">
        <p>A significant fraction of anti-redness serums on the shelf contain menthol, peppermint, eucalyptus, or a similar terpene. The reasoning on the label is intuitive: cool sensation on hot skin, therefore soothing. In the tissue, this is not what is happening.</p>
        <p>Those molecules bind TRPM8 — the cold receptor — but many of them also bind TRPV1, the receptor that reads heat and capsaicin. On rosacea-prone skin, TRPV1 is often the more responsive of the two. What feels like cooling in the first ninety seconds is, at the vessel level, an activation. A few hours of narrowing follow, and then a flush wider than the one before.</p>
        <p>A serum that does anything a flusher can feel — cools, tingles, warms, prickles — is doing something to her receptors. What it feels like is not what it is doing.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="poll" data-poll="four-rooms" role="group" aria-label="Which room triggers your flush?">
