    <section class="entry">
      <p class="entry__object">Object six</p>
      <h2 class="entry__title">The typical count in a flusher's drawer is six to ten.</h2>

      <figure class="entry__photo">
        {%- if s.drawer != blank -%}
          {{ s.drawer | image_url: width: 1200 | image_tag:
               widths: '480, 760, 960, 1200',
               sizes: '(min-width: 960px) 900px, 92vw',
               loading: 'lazy',
               alt: 'Overhead view of an open bathroom drawer holding nine or ten unlabelled skincare tubes, jars and bottles, one amber dropper bottle on its side in the middle.' }}
        {%- endif -%}
        <figcaption>
          <span class="fig-tag">Fig. 4</span>
          <span>A representative drawer, photographed by a reader for a previous investigation. Community-reported treatment counts on the r/Rosacea archive show a median of eight products tried and abandoned per patient before her thirty-fifth birthday.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="entry__body">
        <p>The pattern in a flusher's product drawer is not that any single product failed. Each one worked, briefly, on the specific point in the pathway it was designed to work on. The barrier cream repaired the barrier. The niacinamide serum quieted the vessel. The prescription gel suppressed inflammation.</p>
        <p>None of them addressed the translator between the systems — the specific cell that reads a vessel signal and decides whether to keep the vessel open. That is why the count in the drawer rises. Not because the patient is difficult. Because the target is.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

